  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Meeting the livelihoods needs of disabled people in disaster-prone areas of Bagerhat district</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Cyclone Aila struck across parts of coastal Bangladesh and eastern India in May 2009. Heavy rains triggered by the cyclone raised river levels and burst mud embankments, flooding homes.  Bagerhat was one of the worst-affected districts, with houses, livestock, crops, and infrastructure more extensively damaged even than after Cyclone Sidr in 2007, despite Sidr having done far more damage in overall terms.  Over a year later many people were still struggling to recover from the impact, and people with disabilities living in the affected areas were particularly hard hit, some being forced to resort to begging to survive. This project began in August 2010 to support the livelihood and other basic needs of 150 disabled people and their families. The activities include poultry and livestock rearing, fishery training, provision of materials for shelter repair, and medical treatment support.</narrative>
  </description>
